:: FirstProject {"position":"275,25","size":"100,100"}
<div class="passage-shell" data-scene="office" data-typing="false"><div class="scene-hero" role="img" aria-label="An empty modern office at Amaze."></div><p class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/an-empty-office-5483236/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cottonbro studio / Pexels</a></p><div class="story-copy"><p class="kicker">02 / THE FIRST REQUEST</p><h1>A discount, already selected</h1><p>Morgan opens the checkout for a $22 pack of household supplies. A green badge offers the same pack for <strong>$18 with Sub and Save</strong>.</p><p>The subscription option is selected before the customer touches the page. The smaller text says the item will arrive every month until cancelled.</p><div class="checkout" aria-label="Fictional checkout illustration"><span class="mini-label">AMAZE · CHECKOUT PREVIEW</span><strong>Household essentials</strong><div class="price">$18 <small>with Sub and Save</small></div><div class="selected-option">☑ Monthly subscription selected</div><p>$18 each delivery · repeats every month</p><div class="fake-button">Continue</div><span class="muted">One-time purchase: $22</span></div><blockquote>“Keep the subscription selected,” Morgan says. “Repeat orders make revenue predictable. That helps protect everyone’s jobs.”</blockquote><p>You ask whether customers will realize the discount means signing up. Morgan points at the terms.</p><blockquote>“It’s written there. This is standard optimization.”</blockquote><div class="choices">
[[Accept the project without raising an objection->Implementation]]
[[Question the design and discuss the customer risks->Debate]]
[[Refuse to build this checkout->BadEnding1]]
</div></div></div>


:: Graduation {"position":"150,25","size":"100,100"}
<div class="passage-shell" data-scene="graduation" data-typing="false"><div class="scene-hero" role="img" aria-label="A graduation cap and diploma."></div><p class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/photo-of-person-holding-graduation-cap-and-diploma-7944064/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pavel Danilyuk / Pexels</a></p><div class="story-copy"><p class="kicker">01 / A PLACE TO START</p><h1>The offer</h1><p>You have recently graduated and have been applying for jobs for several months.</p><p>Amaze offers you a junior developer position. You accept because you need a steady income and want work experience.</p><p>On your first morning, Morgan, your software-development manager, walks you through the office.</p><blockquote>“We move quickly here. Show us you can deliver, and there’s room to grow.”</blockquote><p>You need this job to pay rent, and your family is happy that you found work.</p><p>You want to do well in your new role.</p><div class="choices">
[[Meet Morgan and see your first project->FirstProject]]
</div></div></div>


:: HardCancellation [typing] {"position":"775,25","size":"100,100"}
<div class="passage-shell" data-scene="deliveries" data-typing="true"><div class="scene-hero" role="img" aria-label="A delivery van filled with cardboard parcels."></div><p class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/brown-cardboard-boxes-inside-the-van-6169662/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tima Miroshnichenko / Pexels</a></p><div class="story-copy"><p class="kicker">06 / THE CUSTOMER EXPERIENCE</p><h1>Easy to enter. Hard to leave.</h1><p>You move cancellation away from the edit-subscription screen. Customers can change quantity and frequency there, but must open each item individually to stop deliveries.</p><p>The monthly default remains. Morgan calls the lower cancellation rate an improvement.</p><p>Jamie receives another pack. On the account page, he changes the quantity before realizing that doing so does not stop the subscription. He opens another menu, then another.</p><blockquote>“Why can’t I just cancel this?”</blockquote><p>The company reports better retention, but Jamie has paid for an order he did not want.</p><div class="choices">
[[Continue to the consequences->Lawsuit]]
</div></div></div>


:: Implementation [typing] {"position":"650,25","size":"100,100"}
<div class="passage-shell" data-scene="office" data-typing="true"><div class="scene-hero" role="img" aria-label="An empty modern office at Amaze."></div><p class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/an-empty-office-5483236/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cottonbro studio / Pexels</a></p><div class="story-copy"><p class="kicker">05 / ONE MORE CHANGE</p><h1>The numbers look good</h1><p>You build the checkout. Sub and Save is selected by default and the delivery interval begins at one month.</p><p>Before release, Taylor, the product lead, arranges a short customer test. Jamie, a volunteer customer, selects Continue.</p><blockquote>“Eighteen dollars for this order. That works for me.”</blockquote><p>When Taylor asks about the next delivery, Jamie pauses.</p><blockquote>“Next delivery? I only wanted one.”</blockquote><p>You share the result with Morgan. Morgan decides to proceed, saying customers can change the settings.</p><p>After launch, recurring orders increase. Morgan praises your speed. Then comes another request.</p><blockquote>“The higher-ups want better retention. Put cancellation deeper in account settings. Make customers open each subscription separately. Keep the edit screen for quantity and frequency only.”</blockquote><p>You now have direct evidence that some customers misunderstand the commitment.</p><div class="choices">
[[Implement the harder cancellation process->HardCancellation]]
[[Refuse to add cancellation barriers->BadEnding2]]
</div></div></div>


